WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.299
It appears Joaquin Phoenix is out to leave
a few patently unhappy faces on the Warner

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.549
Brothers bosses who financed Joker.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.800
Though the film has danced its way to nearly
a billion dollars at the box office since

00:00:11.809 --> 00:00:17.070
hitting theaters, Phoenix whose entrancing
performance as the deeply disturbed man behind

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:21.930
the iconic Batman villain has driven much
of the film's success is reportedly not quite

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.940
as on board with a return engagement as one
might think.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.640
If his comments in a recent interview with
the Los Angeles Times are any indication,

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.740
his participation in a potential Joker sequel
is far from a given.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:34.940
The actor explained,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.080
"I guess the fear was that you'd get locked
into doing something repeatedly that you don't

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.200
really care about, that doesn't motivate you
or excite you.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.100
Part of the whole attraction to me [of Joker]
was there was no expectation.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.660
I didn't sign a deal to do [more films].

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:49.440
It was a one-off.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:53.320
I wouldn't just do a sequel just because the
first movie is successful.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.380
That's ridiculous."

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.540
The Times interview comes a full month after
the release of Joker, which has become the

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.800
highest grossing R-rated movie of all time,
is on track to join the billion dollar movie

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.380
club in the very near future, and is fully
expected to be a major player come awards

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:07.880
season.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:12.600
Needless to say, success like that has led
to a lot of talk around a franchise-obsessed

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.620
Hollywood about whether or not there will
be a sequel to Joker.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.640
Phoenix who's somehow managed to never appear
in any sort of sequel across a career of nearly

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:25.330
four decades has remained clear that a return
to the twisted mind of Arthur Fleck isn't

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:29.830
out of the question, but he's been just as
adamant that it'll take another truly compelling

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:32.820
story to get him to suit up for any Joker
sequel.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:37.740
It's also pretty clear that simply flashing
some extra green in Phoenix's face won't do

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.510
the actor's statement implies he's definitely
not in it just for the money.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:45.480
Still, he admitted that he had talked a bit
with Joker director Todd Phillips about a

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:46.950
follow-up movie, saying,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.560
"Long before the release or before we had
any idea if it would be successful, we talked

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:52.520
about sequels.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.520
In the second or third week of shooting, I
was like, 'Todd, can you start working on

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:57.220
a sequel?

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:58.720
There's way too much to explore.'

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.080
It was kind of in jest but not really."

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:07.940
"It seemed endless, the possibilities of where
we could go with the character."

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.480
The actor went on to share that he'd even
gone so far as to make mock posters for a

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.900
Joker followup, photoshopping the character
into some of the greatest movies of all time.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:17.900
He explained,

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:21.760
"I basically said, 'You could take this character
and put him in any movie.'

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.580
So I did a photo shoot with the on-set photographer
and we made posters where I photoshopped Joker

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:32.200
into 10 classic movies: Rosemary's Baby, Raging
Bull, Yentl... if you see it, you're like,

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.660
'Yeah, I'd watch that movie.'

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:34.780
Yentl with Joker?

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.360
That would be... amazing!"

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:40.780
Director Todd Phillips seems equally ambivalent
about signing on for a follow-up film.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:45.940
In the same Times piece, he insinuated that
the fate of a Joker 2 may hinge entirely on

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:48.100
the return of both actor and director.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.560
The director admitted that they haven't talked
much about it at all, adding,

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.340
"We've only talked about the fact that if
we ever did one and I'm not saying we are

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:59.780
because right now we're not it couldn't just
be this wild and crazy movie about the 'Clown

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:00.760
Prince of Crime.'

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.600
That just doesn't interest us.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.000
[...] I think the reason why [Joker is] resonating
is what's going on underneath the movie.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.840
So many movies are about the spark, and this
movie is about the powder.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:14.000
If you could capture that again in a real
way, that would be interesting."

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:18.060
The real question becomes whether or not Phoenix
and Phillips can find as much intrigue in

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:21.640
the explosion as they did in the simmering
fuse that is Joker.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:26.020
Either way, the fire is still burning for
more; here's hoping that actor and director

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.920
can both find something interesting in the
flames.

